---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const toc = [
  { id: "why", label: "Why this page exists" },
  { id: "jsr", label: "JSR packages" },
  { id: "npm", label: "npm packages" },
  { id: "rebuild", label: "Rebuild and compare" },
  { id: "binaries", label: "The zuke binary" },
  { id: "limits", label: "What none of this proves" },
];

const auditSnippet = `# In your own project, after installing:
npm audit signatures`;

const rebuildSnippet = `git clone https://github.com/zuke-build/zuke
cd zuke
./zuke npmVerify`;

const binarySnippet = `# The archive is one the release lists, byte for byte:
sha256sum --ignore-missing --check SHA256SUMS

# It was built by Zuke's release workflow, from this repository:
gh attestation verify zuke-<version>-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz \\
  --repo zuke-build/zuke \\
  --signer-workflow zuke-build/zuke/.github/workflows/release.yml \\
  --source-ref refs/heads/master`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Verify what you install"
  description="How to check that a Zuke package or binary was built by Zuke's public release workflow from a public commit, and for npm, that it is exactly what that commit compiles to. Each check says what it proves and what it does not."
  toc={toc}
>
  <p class="lead">
    Zuke runs your build, with your credentials. You should not have to take
    our word for what is in it. Every package and binary we publish can be
    checked against this repository, and for npm you can rebuild it yourself and
    compare the bytes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why">Why this page exists</h2>
  <p>
    Each check below says what it <strong>proves</strong> and what it
    <strong>does not</strong>. Together they answer two questions: who built
    this, and is it what the public source says it is. None of them proves the
    source is free of bugs; that is what code review, the test suite and the
    <a href="https://github.com/zuke-build/zuke/blob/master/SECURITY.md">security policy</a>
    are for.
  </p>

  <h2 id="jsr">JSR packages (<code>@zuke/*</code>)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Provenance.</strong> Every version is published from Zuke's release
      workflow through OIDC, and JSR records a
      <a href="https://www.sigstore.dev/">Sigstore</a> provenance attestation
      for it. Open the version on <a href="https://jsr.io/@zuke/core">jsr.io</a>
      and check its provenance panel: it names
      <code>zuke-build/zuke</code>, the exact commit, and the workflow run.
      <em>Proves</em> which workflow and commit published it.
      <em>Does not prove</em> anything about that commit's content.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Source as published.</strong> JSR packages are the committed
      TypeScript files, with no compile step, so a version can be diffed
      directly against its provenance commit. <em>Proves</em> the files are that
      commit's. <em>Does not prove</em> the commit is sound.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="npm">npm packages (<code>@zuke-build/*</code>)</h2>
  <p>
    On npm, the same packages are compiled to JavaScript with
    <code>tsc</code> before they are published, so there is a build step to
    trust. These checks cover it.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The provenance badge.</strong> npmjs.com shows a "Built and signed
      on GitHub Actions" badge on each version, linking the commit and the
      workflow run. <em>Proves</em> which workflow and commit built it.
      <em>Does not prove</em> the files are what that commit compiles to.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>npm audit signatures</code>.</strong> Run it in your own
      project after installing.
      <Code code={auditSnippet} lang="sh" theme="night-owl" />
      <em>Proves</em> each tarball carries npm's registry signature, and that
      some Sigstore identity signed its provenance. <em>Does not prove</em> the
      identity is Zuke's: npm applies no identity policy. The rebuild below
      checks that.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="rebuild">Rebuild and compare</h2>
  <p>
    <code>npmVerify</code> is a target in Zuke's own build. Run it from any clone
    of the repository; there is no tag to check out:
  </p>
  <Code code={rebuildSnippet} lang="sh" theme="night-owl" />
  <p>For every published package version, it:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Requires an SLSA provenance attestation, and verifies it with Sigstore
      against Zuke's exact identity: the <code>release.yml</code> workflow of
      <code>zuke-build/zuke</code> on <code>master</code>, issued by GitHub. The
      certificate names the commit the package was built from, and that commit
      must be on <code>master</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Checks that the signed statement names the exact bytes npm serves, by
      their sha512.
    </li>
    <li>
      Rebuilds the package from that commit, in a clean checkout of it, with
      that commit's own build scripts and pinned compiler. It installs the
      published version the way npm does (with install scripts off) and
      compares every installed file with the rebuild, byte for byte.
    </li>
    <li>
      Compares the registry's manifest, the copy npm acts on when it installs,
      with the rebuild for every field that changes what npm runs or resolves:
      scripts, dependencies, executables, platform limits, entry points. A
      tampered manifest that adds an install script fails here, even when the
      files match.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <em>Proves</em> the package was built by Zuke's release workflow from a
    commit on <code>master</code>, that the bytes npm serves are the ones that
    workflow signed, and that they are exactly what that commit compiles to.
    Zuke publishes a version to npm only when its package is unchanged since
    its release tag, so the npm and JSR packages of the same version carry the
    same source.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      One exception: the first npm publish, on 20 November 2026, built every
      package from one commit, <code>09d0edc</code>. For a package that changed
      after its release, that npm version carries the newer source rather than
      the JSR files of the same version. Its provenance says so, and
      <code>npmVerify</code> proves exactly that. The same run left a
      <code>0.0.0-stage</code> version on each package: npm's own placeholder
      for staged publishing, a stub with no code that is never
      <code>latest</code>.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    Zuke's release workflow runs the same check after every publish, on Linux,
    macOS and Windows, with no secrets. The <strong>Verify npm packages</strong>
    job's logs are public on the
    <a href="https://github.com/zuke-build/zuke/actions/workflows/release.yml">release workflow</a>.
    That shows the rebuild agrees on three operating systems; running it
    yourself means you do not have to trust our CI.
  </p>

  <h2 id="binaries">The zuke binary</h2>
  <p>
    Every binary release lists the SHA-256 of each archive and carries a
    build-provenance attestation. Homebrew, Chocolatey and the
    <code>./zuke</code> launchers check the pinned SHA-256 on every download. To
    verify one yourself:
  </p>
  <Code code={binarySnippet} lang="sh" theme="night-owl" />
  <p>
    <em>Proves</em> the archive was built by Zuke's release workflow from this
    repository, and is the one the release lists. <em>Does not prove</em> the
    source is sound.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">What none of this proves</h2>
  <p>
    These checks tie what you install to a public commit and a public workflow.
    They do not prove that commit is free of bugs or of a malicious change that
    was reviewed and merged. If a version fails any of these checks, or its
    provenance names another repository or a commit not on
    <code>master</code>, treat it as compromised and report it as the
    <a href="https://github.com/zuke-build/zuke/blob/master/SECURITY.md">security policy</a>
    describes.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
